## Task
When a user clicks "Log In with Passkey" and no passkey is registered to this device/keychain for routefast.app, the browser throws `NotAllowedError` (user cancelled or no credential). Current client shows generic "Login failed. Please try again." which is misleading.

Also handle the server-side "Credential not found" case — the browser returned a credentialId that isn't in DB (stale/orphaned passkey in keychain).

## Changes (`src/app/pages/user/Login.tsx` handlePasskeyLogin)
Distinguish error types:

```ts
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof Error) {
    if (error.name === 'NotAllowedError') {
      setResult("No passkey found for this device. Try password login or reset via email.");
      setAuthMode("password"); // auto-switch to password tab
      return;
    }
  }
  setResult("Passkey login failed. Try password instead.");
  setAuthMode("password");
}
```

And when `finishPasskeyLogin` returns false (credential not in DB):
```ts
if (!success) {
  setResult("This passkey isn't registered for your account. Use password instead.");
  setAuthMode("password");
  return;
}
```

## Test Steps
1. Clear keychain passkeys for routefast.app
2. Click passkey login → user-readable error, auto-switches to password tab
3. With valid passkey → still works

## Context
Observed in prod: logs show "finishPasskeyLogin: Credential not found" for `barrett@digitalglue.dev` and `barrettburnworth@gmail.com`. Likely stale/orphan passkey in keychain from prior failed registration attempt.

## Task
ForgotPassword page shows blank screen on rate-limit 429 and when RESEND_API_KEY is missing. Two server-side issues + one client-side issue.

## Server-side fixes (`src/app/pages/user/passwordReset.ts`)
1. Move `new Resend(workerEnv.RESEND_API_KEY)` **inside** the try block — currently at line 19, outside the try/catch, so a missing/invalid key throws an unhandled error that closes the RSC stream.
2. Guard missing key explicitly:
```ts
if (!workerEnv.RESEND_API_KEY) {
  console.error('RESEND_API_KEY not configured');
  return { success: false, error: 'Email service not configured. Contact support.' };
}
```
3. Remove the silent `return { success: true }` when `!user.passwordHash` (passwordReset.ts:31-34). A user without a password still needs a reset — they can't log in any other way if their passkey fails. Instead: proceed with normal reset flow. The token will set a password on reset.

## Client-side fix (`src/app/pages/user/ForgotPassword.tsx`)
Wrap `requestPasswordReset(email)` call in try/catch. On thrown error (429 returns a non-RSC response, throws on client), show `"Too many reset attempts. Wait 15 minutes and try again."` Never render blank.

```ts
try {
  const response = await requestPasswordReset(email);
  if (response.success) { setSent(true); ... }
  else { setResult(response.error ?? 'Failed to send reset email'); }
} catch (err) {
  setResult('Too many attempts or network error. Try again in a few minutes.');
}
```

## Test Steps
1. pnpm run types
2. Trigger flow with valid email + RESEND_API_KEY set → email received
3. Unset RESEND_API_KEY locally, trigger → clear error message, no blank
4. Trigger 6 times in 15 min from same IP → 429 surfaced as user-readable message

## Task
On step 3 ("check your email") of /user/auth, poll server for session every 3 seconds for up to 10 minutes. If user taps the magic link in the same browser (opens new tab, session created), the waiting tab detects it and redirects to /route/.

## Add auth status endpoint
New route: `GET /api/auth/status` → returns `{ authenticated: boolean }` based on `ctx.user`.

```ts
// in userRoutes or new authApi file
route('/api/auth/status', async ({ ctx }) => {
  return Response.json({ authenticated: !!ctx.user })
})
```

Wire into worker.tsx (top-level, not under /user prefix — so it's `/api/auth/status`).

## Client polling in AuthPage.tsx step 3
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (step !== 'check-email') return
  let elapsed = 0
  const maxMs = 10 * 60 * 1000 // 10 min
  const id = setInterval(async () => {
    elapsed += 3000
    if (elapsed >= maxMs) { clearInterval(id); setPollTimedOut(true); return }
    try {
      const r = await fetch('/api/auth/status')
      const data = await r.json()
      if (data.authenticated) {
        clearInterval(id)
        window.location.href = '/route/'
      }
    } catch {} // ignore transient network errors
  }, 3000)
  return () => clearInterval(id)
}, [step])
```

When `pollTimedOut` is true, swap copy from "Check your email..." to "Done on another device? Refresh this page to continue."

## Test Steps
1. pnpm run types
2. On desktop, request magic link → step 3
3. Open same email in same browser (e.g. Gmail tab) → click link → new tab creates session
4. Within 6s, original tab should redirect to /route/
5. Cross-device scenario: original tab eventually shows "Done on another device?" after 10 min
6. Close email before tap → 10-min timeout, copy swap

## Rate note
Polling generates 200 requests over 10 min per tab. /api/auth/status is cheap (just reads ctx.user, already loaded by worker middleware). Acceptable.

## Task
Route handler at `/user/magic` that reads token from query, calls `verifyMagicLink`, creates session via response headers, and redirects.

## Route (`src/app/pages/user/routes.ts`)
```ts
route('/magic', [
  passwordResetRateLimit, // reuse existing (5/15min/IP) — defeats brute-force token guessing
  MagicLanding,
]),
```

## Create `src/app/pages/user/MagicLanding.tsx` (server component)
```tsx
import { requestInfo } from 'rwsdk/worker'
import { verifyMagicLink } from './magicLink'

export async function MagicLanding() {
  const { request, response } = requestInfo
  const url = new URL(request.url)
  const token = url.searchParams.get('token')

  if (!token) {
    return <MagicErrorPage error="invalid" />
  }

  const result = await verifyMagicLink(token)

  if (!result.success) {
    return <MagicErrorPage error={result.error} />
  }

  // Success: redirect to /route/ (new users) with welcome flag, existing to /route/
  response.headers.set('Location', result.isNewUser ? '/route/?welcome=1' : '/route/')
  return new Response(null, { status: 302, headers: response.headers })
}

function MagicErrorPage({ error }: { error: 'expired' | 'used' | 'invalid' | 'server_error' }) {
  const messages = {
    expired: { title: 'This link expired', body: 'Magic links work for 15 minutes. Request a new one.' },
    used: { title: 'This link was already used', body: 'Sign-in links work once. Request a new one to try again.' },
    invalid: { title: "We couldn't find that link", body: 'The link may be broken. Start over from the sign-in page.' },
    server_error: { title: 'Something went wrong', body: 'Try again, or use password sign-in.' },
  }
  const m = messages[error]
  return (
    <div className="auth-page">
      <div className="auth-container">
        <div className="halftone-shadow" />
        <h1>{m.title}</h1>
        <p>{m.body}</p>
        <a href="/user/auth" className="login-button login-button-primary">Back to sign-in</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  )
}
```

## Capacitor note
On native app intercept (future), /user/magic URL won't hit this web page — app's URL handler calls `/api/auth/magic/verify` instead. Web route is fallback.

